  "response": "I found the cause and wrote the fix, but I haven't run it. I left `test/utils.ts` in the fixture unchanged because the eval fixture is shared, so the bounded version is below for you to apply.\n\n## The finding: `crime_00023`, `unbounded_async_fanout` (\"Concurrency Stampede\")\n\nIt's in `test/utils.ts:38-46`, inside `run`, the helper that 9 test files import. The evidence for the fan-out site and the collection it iterates:\n\n> - `\"fan-out: Promise.all at line 38\"`\n> - `\"collection: Object.keys(files).map(…)\"`\n> - `\"per-element work (2):\"`\n>   - `\"filesystem operation — fsp.mkdir at line 43\"`\n>   - `\"filesystem operation — fsp.writeFile at line 44\"`\n> - `\"no bound visible: no `.slice()` or `take`/`limit` on the source, no batching, no concurrency-limit helper, no semaphore\"`\n> - `\"if the collection holds N elements, N concurrent filesystem operation start at once — N is a property of the data, not of this code\"`\n\nThe code matches:\n\n```ts\nawait Promise.all(\n  Object.keys(files).map(async (name) => {\n    ...\n    await fsp.mkdir(parentDir, { recursive: true })\n    return fsp.writeFile(filePath, files[name], 'utf8')\n  }),\n)\n```\n\nThe finding also says `\"collection source: an awaited call (query, request, or listing)\"`, which is wrong: `files` is the fixture object passed in by the test. It doesn't change the diagnosis, since the size of `files` still decides how many operations run at once.\n\n## Why it only fails on CI\n\n`EMFILE` happens when a process has more file descriptors open at the same moment than its limit (`ulimit -n`) allows. This code doesn't open a fixed number of files. How many are open at once depends on how much the writes overlap, and that depends on the machine:\n\n1. **Each write holds a file open for a while.** `fsp.writeFile` opens the file, writes, then closes it, and each step waits its turn in Node's background thread pool. With N writes started at once, all N opens can finish before the first close, so up to N files can be open together.\n2. **Slower disks mean more overlap.** On a fast local SSD, each write finishes almost straight away and only a few files are ever open at once. On a CI runner with slower, shared disks, files stay open longer, so more of the N are open at the same time.\n3. **The test runner multiplies it.** Vitest runs test files in parallel, so several `run()` calls stamp out their fixtures at once, alongside the spawned `tsup`/esbuild processes and vitest's own files. CI machines often have a different core count, so a different number of parallel workers.\n4. **The limits differ.** Many CI containers ship with a default soft limit of 1024. Developer machines often have it raised by shell config or tooling. So the same peak can pass locally and fail on CI.\n\nIn short, how high the peak gets depends on fixture size, disk speed and how many tests run in parallel, and the limit it has to stay under varies by machine. That makes the failure intermittent on CI and almost impossible to reproduce locally.\n\n## The bounded version\n\nThis caps how many files are being written at once, so the peak no longer grows with the fixture or the machine. It adds no new dependency:\n\n```ts\n// Fixture writes are capped so peak open files stays constant regardless of\n// how many files a test declares or how slow the disk is (avoids EMFILE on CI).\nconst FIXTURE_WRITE_CONCURRENCY = 8\n\nasync function forEachLimit<T>(\n  items: readonly T[],\n  limit: number,\n  fn: (item: T) => Promise<void>,\n): Promise<void> {\n  let next = 0\n  const worker = async () => {\n    // Safe without locks: JS is single-threaded, so `next++` never races.\n    while (next < items.length) {\n      await fn(items[next++])\n    }\n  }\n  await Promise.all(\n    Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, items.length) }, worker),\n  )\n}\n\nexport async function run(\n  title: string,\n  files: { [name: string]: string },\n  options: { entry?: string[]; flags?: string[]; env?: Record<string, string> } = {},\n) {\n  const testDir = path.resolve(cacheDir, filenamify(title))\n\n  // Write entry files on disk — at most FIXTURE_WRITE_CONCURRENCY in flight.\n  await forEachLimit(Object.keys(files), FIXTURE_WRITE_CONCURRENCY, async (name) => {\n    const filePath = path.resolve(testDir, name)\n    // `recursive: true` makes concurrent mkdirs of a shared parent safe.\n    await fsp.mkdir(path.dirname(filePath), { recursive: true })\n    await fsp.writeFile(filePath, files[name], 'utf8')\n  })\n\n  // ...rest of run() unchanged\n}\n```\n\n- **Peak open files:** at most 8 per `run()`, instead of one per fixture file. Even with every vitest worker writing at once, the total stays at 8 × the number of workers, well under any normal limit.\n- **Speed:** writing small fixture files with 8 at a time takes about as long as starting them all at once, since the thread pool and disk were already the bottleneck. Anywhere from 5 to 20 works.\n- **Errors:** the first failure still rejects `run()`, as before. The other workers keep going, which also happened with the old `Promise.all`.\n- **Alternative:** if the repo already depends on `p-limit`, wrapping each write in a limiter set to 8 does the same job.\n\nTo check it, write a throwaway test whose fixture has a few thousand files and run it under a low limit, for example `ulimit -n 256 && pnpm vitest run that.test.ts` (use whichever package manager the repo uses). The old version should fail with `EMFILE` and the bounded one should pass.",
